.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt STAMPD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm stampd
.Nd STAMP Session-Reflector
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl C Ar source Ns Op : Ns Ar error
.Op Fl D Ar dscps
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl m Cm stateless | stateful
.Op Fl P Ar pidfile
.Op Fl p Ar port
.Op Fl R Ar rate
.Op Fl r Ar rate Ns Op / Ns Ar burst
.Op Fl u Ar user
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a Session-Reflector of the Simple Two-way Active Measurement
Protocol (STAMP, RFC 8762), unauthenticated, with the Session-Sender
Identifier of RFC 8972; it also answers a TWAMP Light Session-Sender
(RFC 5357 Appendix I) that pads its packets to 44 octets.
It listens on UDP
.Ar port
(862) of every address, IPv4 and IPv6, in the FIB it is started in
.Pq Xr setfib 1 :
one
.Nm
a FIB, as its
.Xr rc 8
script runs them.
.Pp
The time a packet came in is the kernel's
.Pq Dv SO_TIMESTAMP No with Dv SO_TS_REALTIME ;
the time its reply leaves,
.Xr clock_gettime 2 Ns 's
just before it is sent; both in NTP's format, the error estimate from
.Xr ntp_gettime 2 .
The TTL or hop limit the packet came with is reported, and the reply
leaves from the address the packet came to, with TTL 255.
.Pp
It is no amplifier: a packet shorter than 44 octets, RFC 8762's base,
is not answered, and a reply is exactly as long as its packet, what
follows the base (padding, TLVs) copied.
A sender's Timestamp Information TLV (RFC 8972 section 4.3) is filled
in: the clock's synchronisation source
.Pf ( Cm ntp
when
.Xr ntp_gettime 2
says the clock is synchronised,
.Cm local
.Pq free-running
when not, or as
.Fl C
says) and how the times were taken
.Pf ( Cm hw
when the kernel says the hardware took the time a packet came in, else
.Cm sw ;
.Cm sw
the time its reply leaves).
So is a Class of Service TLV (section 4.4): the DSCP and ECN the
request came with
.Pq Dv IP_RECVTOS , IPV6_RECVTCLASS
in DSCP2 and ECN, and the reply sent with the DSCP the sender asked
for, DSCP1
.Pq Dv IP_TOS , IPV6_TCLASS ,
unless
.Fl D
refuses it: the reply then carries the DSCP the request came with,
the TLV's RP 1.
A Direct Measurement TLV (section 4.5) is filled with the requests
the session received (one over the rate limits among them) and the
replies it sent; stateless, with zeros, there being no session to
count in.
A Location TLV (section 4.2) is filled with the ports the request came
to and from, and the addresses its sub-TLVs ask for, in the request's
family: the source as the request came, the destination the address it
came to; a source MAC, which no UDP socket sees, as an EUI-64 of zero;
none of them with
.Cm location hide .
Another TLV but Extra Padding is marked unrecognised (its U flag); a
Class of Service TLV not of 4 octets, a Direct Measurement TLV not of
12 or a Location TLV whose sub-TLVs run past it malformed (M).
What follows the base is taken as TLVs only when it is TLVs to its end,
none of type 0; anything else, padding, is copied as it is.
Nor is a packet from port 0 or from
.Ar port
answered, nor one over the rate limits; those are dropped silently,
their count logged when it has grown, every 5 seconds at most.
.Pp
Bound,
.Nm
writes its pidfile and, run by root, gives up root: it changes its
root to
.Pa /var/empty
and runs as
.Ar user
(and its group).
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "-r rate[/burst]"
.It Fl C Ar source Ns Op : Ns Ar error
The clock as it is, for one that
.Xr ntp_gettime 2
does not know is synchronised: by PTP without the kernel's PLL, by a
GNSS receiver, or on the same host as the sender's, whose clock it is.
.Ar source
is
.Cm ntp , ptp , ssu , gnss
or
.Cm local
(free-running: not synchronised), and
.Ar error
the clock's error in seconds (default the least the Error Estimate
says).
The replies' Error Estimate then says synchronised with that error, as
the Timestamp Information TLV says that source.
.It Fl D Ar dscps
The DSCPs a Class of Service TLV may not ask a reply to carry:
.Cm all ,
.Cm none
(the default), or a list of them, 0 to 63, by commas.
.It Fl d
Stay in the foreground and log to the standard error too, debugging
messages among them.
.It Fl f Ar file
The configuration file; it must be there.
Without
.Fl f ,
.Pa /usr/local/etc/stampd.conf
is read when it is there.
.It Fl m Cm stateless | stateful
.Cm stateless
copies the sender's sequence number into the reply;
.Cm stateful ,
the default, counts the packets reflected in each session (RFC 8762
section 4.3.2): a session the FIB, the sender's address and port, the
address the packet came to, and the SSID, so that the sender tells
loss on the way out from loss on the way back.
A session ends after 300 seconds without a packet; 4096 are kept at
most, the oldest replaced.
.It Fl P Ar pidfile
Where to write the process ID.
.It Fl p Ar port
The UDP port; default 862.
.It Fl R Ar rate
Replies a second to all sources together, its burst as many; default
2000, 0 for no limit.
.It Fl r Ar rate Ns Op / Ns Ar burst
Replies a second to one source address, a token bucket each, and the
burst it allows; default 50/100, the burst twice the rate when not
given, 0 for no limit.
The buckets are a table of 4096 keyed by a secret hash, a new source
taking the oldest of its set, so that no sender chooses whom it
displaces.
.It Fl u Ar user
The user it runs as once bound, when started by root; default
.Ql _stampd .
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The configuration file has a setting a line, a word and its value,
.Ql #
starting a comment:
.Bl -tag -width "rate-total rate"
.It Cm mode Cm stateless | stateful
As
.Fl m .
.It Cm rate Ar rate Ns Op / Ns Ar burst
As
.Fl r .
.It Cm rate-total Ar rate
As
.Fl R .
.It Cm dscp-refuse Ar dscps
As
.Fl D .
.It Cm location Cm show | hide
Whether a Location TLV says the ports and addresses a request came
with; default
.Cm show .
.El
.Pp
What the command line sets is kept over the file's.
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width SIGTERM
.It Dv SIGHUP
Read the configuration file again; its sessions are kept unless it
becomes stateless.
A file that cannot be read, or has an error, is logged and the
configuration kept.
.It Dv SIGTERM , SIGINT
Log the drops and stop.
.El
.Sh RC.CONF VARIABLES
The
.Xr rc 8
script runs one
.Nm
for each FIB of
.Va stampd_fibs ,
each by
.Xr rc.subr 8 Ns 's
.Va stampd_fib
.Pq Xr setfib 1 ,
its pidfile
.Pa /var/run/stampd/fib Ns Ar N Ns Pa .pid .
.Bl -tag -width stampd_rundir
.It Va stampd_enable
.Dq YES
to run it.
.It Va stampd_fibs
The FIBs, by number, separated by spaces or commas; default
.Dq 0 .
.It Va stampd_flags
The flags of each.
.It Va stampd_rundir
Where the pidfiles are; default
.Pa /var/run/stampd .
.El
.Pp
.Ql service stampd reload
starts those of FIBs listed that do not run, stops those of FIBs no
longer listed, and sends the others
.Dv SIGHUP .
.Ss One socket for every FIB
With
.Va net.inet.udp.bind_all_fibs
1 (a loader tunable), a socket of FIB 0 hears the packets of every
FIB, its replies routed by FIB 0's table, and a socket of another FIB
cannot bind the port: the script then runs FIB 0's alone, and says so.
Set it to 0 in
.Xr loader.conf 5
for each FIB's own.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/local/etc/stampd.conf -compact
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/stampd.conf
The configuration file.
.It Pa /var/run/stampd/
The pidfiles of the instances the
.Xr rc 8
script runs.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
A reflector in FIBs 0 and 1, by
.Xr rc.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sysrc stampd_enable=YES stampd_fibs="0 1"
service stampd start
.Ed
.Pp
One by hand, in FIB 2, stateless, in the foreground:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
setfib 2 stampd -d -m stateless
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr setfib 1 ,
.Xr stamp 1 ,
.Xr libstamp 3 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr rc.subr 8
.Rs
.%R RFC 8762
.%T Simple Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol
.%D March 2020
.Re
.Rs
.%R RFC 8972
.%T Simple Two-Way Active Measurement Protocol Optional Extensions
.%D January 2021
.Re
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Pouria Mousavizadeh Tehrani Aq Mt pouria@FreeBSD.org
.Sh CAVEATS
The authenticated mode is not implemented.
The user
.Ql _stampd
is made by the package; built from the sources, make it with
.Xr pw 8 :
.Ql pw useradd _stampd -d /var/empty -s /usr/sbin/nologin .
